### What is the fragile base class problem?

The fragile base class problem occurs when inheritance exposes parent methods that break the child's contract.

### How does Java's Stack demonstrate the problem?

```java
// java.util.Stack extends Vector: the broken design in the JDK itself
java.util.Stack<String> stack = new java.util.Stack<>();
stack.push("A");
stack.push("B");
stack.push("C");

// Breaks LIFO contract:
stack.add(0, "Z");           // insert at bottom: Vector.add(index, element) leaked
stack.remove(0);             // remove from bottom: Vector.remove(int) leaked
stack.get(1);                // random access: Vector.get(int) leaked
stack.set(0, "REPLACED");    // replace arbitrary position: leaked
// The Stack's LIFO contract is destroyed.
// The Javadoc says: use Deque instead.
```

### How does composition fix this?

```java
package com.bank.util;

import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.EmptyStackException;
import java.util.List;

public class SafeStack<E> {
    private final List<E> elements = new ArrayList<>(); // private: hidden

    public void push(E item) {
        elements.add(item);
    }

    public E pop() {
        if (elements.isEmpty()) throw new EmptyStackException();
        return elements.remove(elements.size() - 1);
    }

    public E peek() {
        if (elements.isEmpty()) throw new EmptyStackException();
        return elements.get(elements.size() - 1);
    }

    public boolean isEmpty() { return elements.isEmpty(); }
    public int size() { return elements.size(); }

    // No add(index, item), no remove(0), no get(index), no set(index, item).
    // LIFO contract enforced. ArrayList is hidden.
}
```

### What does a banking example look like?

```java
// WRONG: inheritance leaks ArrayList methods
public class BrokenPriorityQueue extends ArrayList<Customer> {
    public void enqueue(Customer customer) {
        int index = findInsertionPoint(customer.getPriority());
        add(index, customer);
    }
    public Customer dequeueHighest() {
        if (isEmpty()) throw new java.util.NoSuchElementException();
        return remove(0);
    }
}
// Caller can bypass priority:
// queue.add(0, new Customer("Hacker", Priority.LOWEST)); // front of queue

// CORRECT: composition hides ArrayList
public class SafePriorityQueue {
    private final java.util.List<Customer> queue = new ArrayList<>();
    public void enqueue(Customer customer) {
        int index = findInsertionPoint(customer.getPriority());
        queue.add(index, customer);
    }
    public Customer dequeueHighest() {
        if (queue.isEmpty()) throw new java.util.NoSuchElementException();
        return queue.remove(0);
    }
    // No add(0, customer) exposed. Priority contract enforced.
}
```

### What is the rule for when to inherit vs compose?

If your class IS-A list (it should have all list methods), then extend. If your class USES a list internally but is NOT a list (stack, queue, priority queue), then compose.

### What are common mistakes?

The most common mistake is inheriting from a collection class for storage reuse.

```java
// WRONG: inheriting for code reuse
public class AccountCache extends HashMap<String, Account> { }
// Exposes all HashMap methods: callers can call remove() to bypass cache invalidation.

// CORRECT: composition
public class AccountCache {
    private final java.util.Map<String, Account> cache = new java.util.HashMap<>();
    public Account get(String id) { return cache.get(id); }
    public void put(String id, Account account) {
        cache.put(id, account);
        notifyCacheUpdate(id); // controlled side effect
    }
}
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

- The JDK added Deque (interface with push/pop) instead of fixing Stack, because Stack extends Vector and removing that inheritance would break backward compatibility.
- Effective Java Item 18: favor composition over inheritance, especially when you do not control the parent class.
- In Spring, services never extend collection classes. They compose repositories and other services as fields.
